! fcc nickel with U = 3 eV, started from a small moment -- the DFT+U counterpart
! of benchmarks/fe-unstable.in, and a capability case rather than a timing one.
!
! There are two self-consistent solutions here: a ferromagnetic one and a
! non-magnetic one that is a *saddle* of the energy in the magnetization
! direction. Perturb the non-magnetic solution by 2% along that direction and
! damped mixing runs away to the ferromagnet, while a Newton method returns to
! it -- which is what "unstable fixed point" means, and what makes the saddle
! the reference state a magnetic stabilisation energy is quoted against.
!
! **Reaching it needs a custom starting occupation matrix, not just a small
! starting_magnetization.** init_ns fills ns diagonally by Hund's rule, so the
! default start is 1.0 in every majority d orbital against 0.8 in every minority
! one whether starting_magnetization is 0.7 or 0.05 -- already deep in the
! ferromagnetic basin. run_scf(starting_ns=...) with pypresso.hubbard.uniform_ns
! is how the unpolarised start is asked for. See PLAN.md P22 and
! tests/regression/test_scf_solvers.py.
!
! One k-point and nosym as in ni-ldau-1k.in, so this runs in seconds; no number
! here is a converged physics claim.
 &control
    calculation = 'scf'
 /
 &system
    ibrav = 2, celldm(1) = 6.48, nat = 1, ntyp = 1,
    ecutwfc = 24.0, ecutrho = 288.0,
    nspin = 2, starting_magnetization(1) = 0.05,
    occupations = 'smearing', smearing = 'marzari-vanderbilt', degauss = 0.02
    nosym = .true.
 /
 &electrons
    conv_thr = 1.0d-8
 /
ATOMIC_SPECIES
 Ni 58.69 Ni.pz-nd-rrkjus.UPF
ATOMIC_POSITIONS (alat)
 Ni 0.0 0.0 0.0
K_POINTS
 1
   0.1250000  0.1250000  0.1250000   1.00
HUBBARD {ortho-atomic}
U Ni-3d 3.0
